  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` meets the built-in's criteria.\n\n- **Placement:** The function is an explicit function with a body. The explanation sits in fenced citation commentary, so it counts as tracked justification.\n- **Substance:** The function is a guard that always panics. The commentary says it must stay unexecuted, because running it would mean the storage-only test did an unwanted graph access. Difficulty is not the stated reason, so this is substantive.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - The `Graph` trait in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs` declares `outgoing_edges` as a required method, so `StoredGraph` has to define it.\n  - The delegation test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` reaches `StoredGraph` only through `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`. Those call `self.node_path(node)` and never `outgoing_edges`, so the guard is never run.\n  - Other fixtures implement `outgoing_edges` with real bodies, in `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]` and `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs#impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[outgoing_edges]`. This supports the claim that traversal fixtures exercise edge discovery.\n- **Other rules:** There is no redundant `{impl}` or `{test}` reference on this item. The citation is a built-in, so the project-requirement role procedure does not apply.",
